de】 · 【115†Measured profiles】 · 【100†Formal verification】 · 【116†Refinement evidence】\nL236: Its SMT frontend proves membership, non-membership and single-leaf updates. Models of the SMT circuit compiler and of the GKR and sumcheck protocol, and their composition, are machine-checked in Isabelle/HOL, building on the Archive of Formal Proofs `Sumcheck_Protocol` entry and including the exact degree-four KoalaBear extension field that the implementation uses.\nL237: A conditional, mechanized forward acceptance theorem and recorded proofs for selected Rust contracts relate the model to the shipped verifier: the checks that turn a request into an acceptance verdict (public-input binding, sumcheck rounds, carry construction and final input evaluations) can be traced across model and code; see 【116†Refinement evidence and reuse】.\nL238: Prepare a circuit once, reuse its proving and verification material, and run independent CPU proving jobs in parallel. The protocol and sumcheck crates have no dependency on the SMT compiler, so other arithmetic-circuit frontends can build on the same engine and use its 【116†acceptance-check review map】 when reviewing an integration.\nL239: ## Implementation benefits\nL240: 【117†Image: StateSync-GKR v1.2.0 in four measured cells, each with its own baseline: 89-fold less peak memory than the engine's own dense baseline on a width-4,096 test circuit (89.46-fold on v1.1.0, 88.87-fold on v1.2.0); 6,470-fold faster proving than that baseline on the same circuit, against 2,507-fold with v1.1.0; 3,300 requests a second on one 192-core server with every request accepted within one second, against 1,150 for v1.1.0 measured the same day; and a 99th-percentile latency of 34 ms at 1,000 requests a second on the same server, against 440 ms for v1.1.0 the same day.】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.seas.upenn.edu/~l